From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Wed, 10 Sep 2026 16:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 76/76] local: thermal/qcom-spmi-temp-alarm: dump the alarm and
 interrupt state on hibernate restore

Pure instrumentation. Reads nine registers, writes none, changes no
behaviour. It exists because three fix attempts for this bug were wrong and
the next step has to be evidence.

The bug: after a cross-boot hibernate resume all eight PMIC temp-alarm
interrupts assert continuously, ~2400/s each, all dispatched in one
pmic_arb_chained_irq() pass, pinning cpu0 at ~90% hardirq until the machine
is unusable. There is no thermal event behind it -- every zone still reports
DEFAULT_TEMP, and qpnp_tm_update_temp_no_adc() only moves chip->temp when
get_temp_stage() changes, so the stage has not moved since probe.

What has been tried, and what it ruled out:

  - Cycling the driver binding (unbind + rebind) stops the storm dead on the
    cycled alarm while the other seven keep going. So *something* done at
    probe fixes it.
  - Re-running qpnp_tm_init() from a .restore -- trip thresholds plus
    ALARM_CTRL_FORCE_ENABLE -- does NOT fix it. With the call succeeding
    (rev2, after fixing a chip->ntrips idempotency bug that made rev1 fail
    -EINVAL) the storm ran at 4064/s per line versus 2371/s with no patch at
    all, i.e. reprogramming the alarm makes it assert harder.

The remaining difference between those two is that a rebind RE-REQUESTS THE
IRQ. spmi-pmic-arb declares no PM ops at all, and the QPNP per-interrupt
registers are written only at request time: qpnpint_irq_domain_activate()
writes EN_CLR and LATCHED_CLR, irq_unmask() writes EN_SET, irq_set_type()
writes SET_TYPE/POLARITY_HIGH/POLARITY_LOW. Across a hibernate the PMIC comes
back from a reboot while irq_desc comes back from the image, so none of it is
rewritten.

That is a hypothesis, not a conclusion -- and qpnpint_irq_unmask() only
rewrites hardware when EN_SET reads back clear, so simply replaying the irq
setup may be a no-op too. Rather than guess a fourth time, read the state.

Those registers live in this peripheral's own block (0x10-0x18), which
qpnp_tm_read() already reaches via chip->base, so no new plumbing is needed.
Dump them at probe for a healthy reference and again on .restore, and diff.

RT_STS is the discriminator: set means the alarm source genuinely is
asserting and the fault is in the alarm block; clear, with LATCHED_STS
setting anyway, means the line is being re-latched by an edge/polarity
configuration that no longer matches what Linux believes -- which points at
spmi-pmic-arb and not at this driver.

platform_set_drvdata() is added because the driver never stored it; get_temp
reaches the chip through thermal_zone_device_priv().

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/thermal/qcom/qcom-spmi-temp-alarm.c | 71 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 71 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/thermal/qcom/qcom-spmi-temp-alarm.c
+++ b/drivers/thermal/qcom/qcom-spmi-temp-alarm.c
@@ -755,6 +755,73 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Diagnostic only -- reads, never writes.
+ *
+ * After a cross-boot hibernate resume all eight PMIC temp-alarm interrupts
+ * assert continuously (~2400/s each, cpu0 pinned in hardirq) with no thermal
+ * event behind them: every zone still reports DEFAULT_TEMP, so
+ * get_temp_stage() has not changed since probe. Cycling the driver binding
+ * stops it dead; re-running qpnp_tm_init() from a .restore does not, and
+ * makes it worse. The difference is that a rebind re-requests the irq, and
+ * the QPNP per-interrupt registers are programmed only at request time.
+ *
+ * Those registers live in this peripheral's own block, so dump them here and
+ * compare probe (healthy) against restore (storming). RT_STS is the
+ * discriminator: set means the source really is asserting, clear means the
+ * line is being re-latched by an edge/polarity configuration that no longer
+ * matches what Linux believes.
+ */
+#define QPNP_TM_REG_INT_RT_STS		0x10
+#define QPNP_TM_REG_INT_SET_TYPE	0x11
+#define QPNP_TM_REG_INT_POLARITY_HIGH	0x12
+#define QPNP_TM_REG_INT_POLARITY_LOW	0x13
+#define QPNP_TM_REG_INT_EN_SET		0x15
+#define QPNP_TM_REG_INT_LATCHED_STS	0x18
+
+static void qpnp_tm_dump_state(struct qpnp_tm_chip *chip, const char *when)
+{
+	static const struct { u16 addr; const char *name; } regs[] = {
+		{ QPNP_TM_REG_STATUS,		 "status"    },
+		{ QPNP_TM_REG_SHUTDOWN_CTRL1,	 "shdn_ctl1" },
+		{ QPNP_TM_REG_ALARM_CTRL,	 "alarm_ctl" },
+		{ QPNP_TM_REG_INT_RT_STS,	 "rt_sts"    },
+		{ QPNP_TM_REG_INT_SET_TYPE,	 "set_type"  },
+		{ QPNP_TM_REG_INT_POLARITY_HIGH, "pol_hi"    },
+		{ QPNP_TM_REG_INT_POLARITY_LOW,	 "pol_lo"    },
+		{ QPNP_TM_REG_INT_EN_SET,	 "en_set"    },
+		{ QPNP_TM_REG_INT_LATCHED_STS,	 "latched"   },
+	};
+	char buf[160];
+	int i, n = 0;
+	u8 val;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(regs); i++) {
+		if (qpnp_tm_read(chip, regs[i].addr, &val) < 0)
+			n += scnprintf(buf + n, sizeof(buf) - n, " %s=??",
+				       regs[i].name);
+		else
+			n += scnprintf(buf + n, sizeof(buf) - n, " %s=%02x",
+				       regs[i].name, val);
+	}
+
+	dev_info(chip->dev, "%s:%s stage=%u\n", when, buf, chip->stage);
+}
+
+static int __maybe_unused qpnp_tm_pm_restore(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct qpnp_tm_chip *chip = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+
+	if (chip)
+		qpnp_tm_dump_state(chip, "restore");
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct dev_pm_ops qpnp_tm_pm_ops = {
+	.restore = pm_sleep_ptr(qpnp_tm_pm_restore),
+};
+
 static int qpnp_tm_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct qpnp_tm_chip *chip;
@@ -883,6 +950,9 @@
 
 	thermal_zone_device_update(chip->tz_dev, THERMAL_EVENT_UNSPECIFIED);
 
+	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, chip);
+	qpnp_tm_dump_state(chip, "probe");
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -896,6 +966,7 @@
 	.driver = {
 		.name = "spmi-temp-alarm",
 		.of_match_table = qpnp_tm_match_table,
+		.pm = pm_sleep_ptr(&qpnp_tm_pm_ops),
 	},
 	.probe  = qpnp_tm_probe,
 };
